From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Shell overlays follow the appearance mode, not color-scheme

The desktop's right-click menu, the search overlay, and every extension
popover (the tiling picker among them) stayed dark in light mode. Nick's
session prefers dark (sessionMode.colorScheme is 'prefer-dark'), and
Settings' surface-treatment panel writes G_DESKTOP_COLOR_SCHEME_DEFAULT for
light, frost and glass alike -- only its own dark toggle writes prefer-dark.
getStyleVariant() resolved a color-scheme of 'default' under a
session that prefers dark to the dark variant sheet, so every Shell surface
the variant sheet owns (.popup-menu-content, .search-entry, .search-section,
and by extension any third-party extension popover) loaded dark literals
regardless of the chosen treatment. Application windows never had this
problem: the app kit reads the same 'default' color-scheme as light directly,
with no session preference in between.

Every bound surface -- the Shelf, Quick Options, notifications, the Well --
was already correct, because SurfaceMaterialController stamps a
luma-surface-{light,dark,frost,glass} class from Luma's own effective
treatment and 0150's ledger reads that class. The menus and the search
overlay are the one path in the Shell that never asked the treatment
controller anything: they inherit whatever base variant sheet main.js
happened to load.

lumaSurfaceMaterials.js exports effectiveTreatment() (evaluated fresh, not
cached) and connectTreatmentChanged(callback). getStyleVariant() now asks it
directly for 'prefer-dark'/'prefer-light' sessions instead of reading
color-scheme, and main.js and extensionSystem.js both reload their
stylesheets when the treatment changes, the same way they already reload on
a color-scheme or high-contrast change. force-dark and force-light session
modes (the greeter, the lock screen) and high-contrast are unaffected: they
never consulted color-scheme and still don't.

Frost and glass are smoked surfaces carrying white ink (0150), so they build
on the dark variant sheet and let their own luma-surface rules re-tint what
they bind -- unchanged from before. That is the one place a Shell surface
still deliberately loads the dark sheet outside of an explicit dark choice,
and it is stated here rather than left to be rediscovered: everything the
dark sheet gives a bound frost/glass surface for free (dark literals meant to
sit under a translucent smoked veil) is exactly what 0150's ledger overrides.
An *unbound* surface under frost/glass -- if one exists -- would see the raw
dark sheet, which is why this patch is paired with a survey of every Shell
overlay rather than trusted on inspection alone.

Surveyed and confirmed following the mode through this one mechanism, with no
per-surface binding needed because their chrome comes from the base variant
sheet: the desktop's right-click menu (backgroundMenu.js, PopupMenu.PopupMenu
base), the dock's right-click menu (same base), Quick Options and the Well
(already bound, unaffected by this patch, listed to say they were checked),
and the search overlay (search.js, .search-entry/.search-section/
.list-search-result-* in the variant sheet).

Not fixed here, and said plainly rather than silently dropped: the Tiling
Shell layout picker (gnome-shell-extension-tiling-shell). Its extension
stylesheet now reloads on a treatment change exactly like every other
extension's, through the mechanism this patch adds -- but whether that does
anything depends on whether its stylesheet.css itself has light/dark rules to
switch between, which needs the extension's own source. It is fetched from a
zip at build time and is not vendored in this repository; the build host and
the ThinkPad, the only two places that source or an installed copy exist,
were both unreachable for the whole of this work. If Luma's fork of Tiling
Shell has no light-mode rules of its own, the fix belongs in its own patch
series, not here, and needs that source read first.

Generated against the Shell .109 tree (0151 applied) for release .110.
---
diff --git a/js/ui/main.js b/js/ui/main.js
--- a/js/ui/main.js
+++ b/js/ui/main.js
@@ -54,6 +54,7 @@
 import {formatError} from '../misc/errorUtils.js';
 import * as ParentalControlsManager from '../misc/parentalControlsManager.js';
 import * as Util from '../misc/util.js';
+import * as LumaSurfaceMaterials from './lumaSurfaceMaterials.js';
 
 const WELCOME_DIALOG_LAST_SHOWN_VERSION = 'welcome-dialog-last-shown-version';
 // Make sure to mention the point release, otherwise it will show every time
@@ -189,6 +190,10 @@
 
     St.Settings.get().connect('notify::high-contrast', _loadDefaultStylesheet);
     St.Settings.get().connect('notify::color-scheme', _loadDefaultStylesheet);
+    // Luma's appearance mode decides the variant (see getStyleVariant), and
+    // it can change without color-scheme changing -- Settings writes the same
+    // `default` for light, frost and glass.
+    LumaSurfaceMaterials.connectTreatmentChanged(_loadDefaultStylesheet);
 
     // Initialize ParentalControlsManager before the UI
     ParentalControlsManager.getDefault();
@@ -511,18 +516,28 @@
 
 /** @returns {string} */
 export function getStyleVariant() {
-    const {colorScheme} = St.Settings.get();
     switch (sessionMode.colorScheme) {
     case 'force-dark':
         return 'dark';
     case 'force-light':
         return 'light';
     case 'prefer-dark':
-        return colorScheme === St.SystemColorScheme.PREFER_LIGHT
-            ? 'light' : 'dark';
-    case 'prefer-light':
-        return colorScheme === St.SystemColorScheme.PREFER_DARK
-            ? 'dark' : 'light';
+    case 'prefer-light':
+        // Luma's appearance mode is the one source of truth for the Shell's
+        // variant, not GNOME's color-scheme. The user session prefers dark, so
+        // upstream resolved a color-scheme of `default` to the dark sheet --
+        // and `default` is what Settings writes for the light treatment. Every
+        // overlay the variant sheet owns (the desktop menu, search, extension
+        // pickers) therefore stayed dark in light mode, while the apps, which
+        // read `default` as light, did not.
+        //
+        // The effective treatment already folds in color-scheme when no
+        // treatment is chosen, and high contrast and the renderer fallbacks,
+        // so nothing upstream relied on is lost. Frost and glass are smoked
+        // surfaces carrying white ink, so they build on the dark sheet and
+        // their own luma-surface rules re-tint what they bind.
+        return LumaSurfaceMaterials.effectiveTreatment() === 'light'
+            ? 'light' : 'dark';
     default:
         return '';
     }
diff --git a/js/ui/extensionSystem.js b/js/ui/extensionSystem.js
--- a/js/ui/extensionSystem.js
+++ b/js/ui/extensionSystem.js
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 import * as FileUtils from '../misc/fileUtils.js';
 import * as Main from './main.js';
 import * as MessageTray from './messageTray.js';
+import * as LumaSurfaceMaterials from './lumaSurfaceMaterials.js';
 
 const ENABLED_EXTENSIONS_KEY = 'enabled-extensions';
 const DISABLED_EXTENSIONS_KEY = 'disabled-extensions';
@@ -43,6 +44,10 @@
 
         St.Settings.get().connect('notify::color-scheme',
             () => this._reloadExtensionStylesheets());
+        // Extension variant sheets follow the same appearance mode as the
+        // Shell's own, or a light-mode extension popover stays dark.
+        LumaSurfaceMaterials.connectTreatmentChanged(
+            () => this._reloadExtensionStylesheets());
 
         Main.sessionMode.connect('updated', () => {
             this._sessionUpdated().catch(logError);
diff --git a/js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js b/js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js
--- a/js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js
+++ b/js/ui/lumaSurfaceMaterials.js
@@ -121,6 +121,10 @@
 class SurfaceMaterialController {
     constructor() {
         this._bindings = new Set();
+        // Anything that has to follow the appearance mode without being a
+        // bound surface -- the Shell's variant stylesheet, extension
+        // stylesheets -- listens here, so there is one source of truth.
+        this._listeners = new Set();
         this._surfaceSettings = optionalSettings(
             'org.project_luma.shell-state', 'surface-treatment');
         this._interfaceSettings = optionalSettings(
@@ -172,6 +176,7 @@
     }
 
     _sync() {
+        const previous = this._effective;
         this._effective = this._effectiveTreatment();
         const recipe = RECIPES[this._effective];
 
@@ -188,9 +193,26 @@
 
         for (const binding of this._bindings)
             binding._sync(this._effective, recipe);
+
+        if (previous !== undefined && previous !== this._effective) {
+            for (const listener of this._listeners) {
+                try {
+                    listener(this._effective);
+                } catch (error) {
+                    logError(error, 'Unable to follow the Luma appearance mode');
+                }
+            }
+        }
     }
 
     bind(actor, options = {}) {
+        // The controller can now be built early, from main.js choosing its
+        // variant stylesheet, before the renderer can answer whether it is
+        // hardware accelerated. Re-evaluate when the first real surface binds,
+        // so an early answer is never the one the Shell keeps; if it changed,
+        // the listeners reload the stylesheet to match.
+        if (this._bindings.size === 0)
+            this._sync();
         const binding = new SurfaceMaterialBinding(this, actor, options);
         this._bindings.add(binding);
         binding._sync(this._effective, RECIPES[this._effective]);
@@ -291,3 +313,32 @@
     controller ??= new SurfaceMaterialController();
     return controller.bind(actor, options);
 }
+
+/**
+ * The appearance mode the Shell is actually showing, after the accessibility
+ * and renderer fallbacks: 'light', 'dark', 'frost' or 'glass'.
+ *
+ * This, not GNOME's color-scheme, decides which variant stylesheet the Shell
+ * and its extensions load. The two used to disagree: the user session prefers
+ * dark, so a color-scheme of `default` -- which is what Settings writes for
+ * the light treatment -- loaded the dark stylesheet, and every overlay the
+ * variant sheet owns stayed dark in light mode.
+ *
+ * @returns {string}
+ */
+export function effectiveTreatment() {
+    controller ??= new SurfaceMaterialController();
+    // Evaluated fresh, never from the cache, so a stylesheet chosen before the
+    // first surface bound still reflects the renderer as it is now.
+    return controller._effectiveTreatment();
+}
+
+/**
+ * Call `callback(treatment)` whenever the effective appearance mode changes.
+ *
+ * @param {function(string):void} callback
+ */
+export function connectTreatmentChanged(callback) {
+    controller ??= new SurfaceMaterialController();
+    controller._listeners.add(callback);
+}
